Files contain:

#pulse = thermal pulse number 
Mcore = H-exhausted core mass (Msun), sampled at start of thermal pulse 
M_csh = max. extent of convective zone in He-shell (Msun), calculated during a thermal pulse
t_csh = duration of convection in He-shell (years), calculated during a thermal pulse
Ddredge = amount of material dredged from He-shell to envelope (Msun), calculated during a thermal pulse
lambda = third dredge-up efficiency parameter
DMh = core mass growth between current thermal pulse and previous (Msun)
THeshell = max. temperature in the He-shell (K)
Tbce = max. temperature at the base of the envelope during the preceeding interpulse phase (K)
THshell = max. temperature in the H-shell during the preceeding interpulse phase (K)
interpulse = interpulse period (years)
Mtot = total mass (Msun), sampled at start of thermal pulse
MaxL = max. surface luminosity (Lsun), from previous interpulse period
MaxLHe = max. He-shell luminosity (Lsun), from thermal pulse
maxR = max. radius of star (Rsun), from previous interpulse period
Mbol = min. bolometric luminosity, from previous interpulse period
Teff = min. effective temperature (K), from previous interpulse period

Note that these quantities are sampled from one snapshot in time so don't reflect the
variation in R, L, Mcore, Mbol, Teff etc as a function of time during the thermal pulse
cycle. A plot showing the variation in radius for a 2Msun, Z = 0.014 model (with Y=0.28)
is included for comparsion. This plot shows that the radius at the end of the star dramatically
increases at the end, when convergence difficulties occurred and the code crashed during
a thermal pulse. This is close to when the star became C-rich (T = 1.1857e9 years).
